  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nNot much change to the overall thinking for the northwest\nflow/lake enhancement setup through the weekend. Deep Great\nLakes troughing lingers into Sunday, when the parent upper low\nopens up and moves off of the New England coast. Northwest flow\nbetween this and slowly advancing Plains surface high pressure\nwill foster periods of lake effect/enhanced snow and rain\nshowers as minor ripples move through the overall flow. Low-\nlevel flow remains out of a more northerly direction into this\nevening, favoring eastern Ohio initially (although north-to-\nsouth sprinkles/flurries are noted across far western PA). Flow\nthen backs towards more of a NW/WNW pattern later tonight, with\nmore of a \"classic\" radar presentation expected by Saturday,\nwith more activity in western PA and the ridge zones as compared\nto OH. Overall coverage will be higher during the daylight \nhours thanks to diurnal steepening of low-level lapse rates, \nalthough \"heating\" will be a relative term with max temperatures\nlikely coming in 5 to 10 degrees below climatology both \nSaturday and Sunday.\n\nThere is still a bit of uncertainty regarding where more\npersistent showers/bands may set up, with the various REFS\nmembers suggesting differing solutions. Still, the overall\nlimiting factors of a still warm ground (even the higher\nelevations generally only saw grassy accumulation last night)\nand minimal moisture penetration into the DGZ will limit \naccumulation efficiency. Probabilities of greater than 0.5\" of\ntotal snow accumulation through Sunday in the lowlands top out \nbetween 25 and 50 percent, confined to areas north of I-80 and \nfavored mainly during the nighttime hours with some instances \nof melting during daylight. The Laurels and the WV ridges, with \nthe addition of upslope flow, may see a bit of lift enhancement \nand of course longer periods of more snow and less rain due to slightly\ncooler temperatures, although the same moisture limitations \nwill be in place. Here, that 25 to 50 percent range is \napplicable to total accumulation of around an inch through \nSunday, with lower (10-20%) chances of 2 inches on the highest\npeaks. Elsewhere, although snowflakes may mix in from time to \ntime, anything more than a transient nighttime dusting on grassy\nsurfaces is not expected.\n\nCoverage on Sunday will be a bit less than Saturday as the\ntrough begins to exit to the east, which will also coincide with\na slight moderation in temperature.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nPrecipitation cuts off Sunday night as the trough exits and as\nflow backs towards the west, cutting off the lake enhancement\nmachine. Clouds should slowly erode on Monday, leading to\nseasonably chilly lows in the 20s. Dry weather returns across \nthe board into at least the middle of next week with \ntemperatures eventually moderating back to above-normal values \nas the airmass modifies in weak southerly flow.",
